<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lapua Movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a>–<a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lapua Movement was a Finnish radical nationalist and anti-communist movement founded in the town of Lapua in Ostrobothnia in late 1929. It pressed the government into banning communist activity and kidnapped opponents, among them the former president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/k-j-stahlberg">K. J. Ståhlberg</a> in 1930. After its supporters' failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mantsala-rebellion">Mäntsälä rebellion</a> of 1932 it was banned, and its successor was the Patriotic People's Movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lapua-movement.jpg" alt="Lapua Movement" width="320" height="278" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: qWerk · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lapuan_liike.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1929 communist activity rose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> after the 6th World Congress of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Communist International</a> ordered communist parties to accelerate class struggle. A general strike declared in November drew only 5 to 10 per cent of workers. Late that month the Young Communist League of Finland held meetings and protests in Lapua, in Ostrobothnia, a conservative and religious town in a region that had been a stronghold of the White army in the Civil War.</p><p>Nationalists held that the communists had mocked God, the Lutheran Church, the fatherland, the Finnish army and General Mannerheim, and townspeople put a violent end to the meetings. The movement saw itself as the defender of what the Civil War had won, and stood for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>, Finnish nationalism and anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>. It was led by Vihtori Kosola.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 1 December 1929 an anti-communist meeting of more than 1,000 people demanded an end to all communist activity, and a national assembly of about two thousand people drew up demands to remove communists from Parliament and the military and to ban speech that desecrated the foundations of the Finnish people's morality. Many politicians and officers were at first sympathetic. The movement used kidnapping, beating and the practice of driving victims to the Soviet border; many of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> thus deported were later caught up in Stalin's Great Purge. On the night of 28 March 1930 thirteen men destroyed the presses of the communist paper Työn Ääni, and the trial at Vaasa turned into a riot.</p><p>Under pressure, Parliament passed the Communist Laws in 1930, and the former prime minister P. E. Svinhufvud, whom the movement respected, became prime minister on 2 July. About 12,600 men took part in the Peasant March to Helsinki on 7 July. Abductions continued, and in the autumn Onni Happonen was killed, though he was not in fact a communist. The movement also intimidated voters before the election. The kidnapping of the former president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/k-j-stahlberg">Kaarlo Juho Ståhlberg</a> and his wife Ester on 14 October 1930 collapsed general support, and moderates left.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In February 1932 armed Lapua activists broke up a Social Democrat meeting at Mäntsälä, and the event grew into an attempted coup led by General Wallenius. The army and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-movement">White Guards</a> stayed largely loyal to the government, and the rebellion ended after a radio speech by President Svinhufvud. The movement was banned on 21 November 1932 under the Protection of the Republic Act, which it had itself worked to pass, and Wallenius and about 50 other leaders were sentenced to prison. The Patriotic People's Movement was formed soon afterwards, also nationalist and anti-communist, and was banned in 1944 on the orders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continuation-war">Continuation War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lapua Movement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The early republic faced threats from both sides: the Communist Party was banned until 1944, and the anti-communist <b>Lapua Movement</b>, which kidnapped opponents, ended after the failed Mäntsälä rebellion of 1932.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Fascist movements elsewhere took up the tune in the 1930s and 1940s: the British Union of Fascists, the Spanish Falange, the Legion of French Volunteers Against Bolshevism in Vichy France, the All-Russian Fascist Organisation of émigrés in the United States and Finland's <b>Lapua Movement</b> and Patriotic People's Movement.</blockquote><a href="/songs/horst-wessel-lied">Horst-Wessel-Lied</a></li></ul></section>
